{ id: 'opqb-t26-1', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-cornea',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'The patient will fail to blink in case of?',
  options: ['Herpes simplex','Conjunctivitis','Cataract','Glaucoma'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-1', imgAlt: 'Colour head-and-shoulders clinical photograph of a woman, with a gloved hand holding a folded tissue near the outer corner of one eye.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise loss of the corneal (blink) reflex as a sign of neurotrophic corneal disease and identify herpes simplex as its classic cause.',
  explanation: 'The corneal (blink) reflex is elicited by lightly touching the cornea; the afferent limb runs via the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve and the efferent limb via the facial nerve, producing bilateral blinking. Loss of this reflex means loss of corneal sensation, i.e. neurotrophic keratopathy. Among the causes the cached Cornea lecture lists for neurotrophic keratopathy is herpes simplex virus infection, alongside herpes zoster, acoustic neuroma surgery, diabetes mellitus and topical anaesthetic abuse — recurrent or chronic HSV keratitis damages corneal nerve endings, blunting or abolishing corneal sensation and, with it, the reflex blink to a touch stimulus. This is the mechanism the printed key selects.\nConjunctivitis (B) inflames the conjunctiva but does not, as a rule, denervate the cornea; a patient with simple conjunctivitis still blinks normally to a corneal touch. Cataract (C) is a lens opacity with no effect on corneal innervation or the reflex arc at all. Glaucoma (D) raises intraocular pressure and can produce corneal oedema, but it does not classically abolish corneal sensation or the blink reflex, and it is not among the recognised causes of neurotrophic keratopathy in the cached material.\nThe precise physiology of the corneal reflex test itself (afferent trigeminal, efferent facial) is standard neuro-ophthalmic anatomy not itself detailed in the cached exam-technique material — not taken from the course material — but the link between herpes simplex and loss of corneal sensation is directly sourced from the Cornea lecture\'s neurotrophic keratopathy section.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.151, Q1 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` (neurotrophic keratopathy; causes: HSV, HZV, acoustic neuroma surgery, DM, topical anaesthetic abuse).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-2', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-uvea',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'Identify the condition:',
  options: ['Chronic iridocyclitis','Acute anterior uveitis','Acute glaucoma','Scleritis'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-2', imgAlt: 'Colour slit-lamp anterior-segment photograph of one eye showing the iris and an irregularly shaped pupil.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise an irregular, festooned pupil from posterior synechiae as a sign of chronic (recurrent) iridocyclitis rather than a single acute attack.',
  explanation: 'The pupil in this photograph is not the smooth round outline of a normal or acutely inflamed eye; it is scalloped and irregularly lobed, with parts of its margin pulled out of shape. This scalloped, multi-lobed pupil outline is the classic appearance of posterior synechiae — adhesions between the iris and the anterior lens capsule that fix parts of the pupil margin so it can no longer dilate uniformly. The cached Uvea and Sclera lecture illustrates this same festooned-pupil appearance under anterior uveitis sequelae, and the cached Uveal Tract book chapter explicitly ties an irregular iris shape to posterior synechiae, noting cycloplegic-mydriatic treatment is given "to prevent and break recently formed posterior synechiae" — implying established, multiple synechiae of the kind shown here reflect disease that has been present and recurring, not a single acute episode, which is why the key selects the chronic form.\nAcute anterior uveitis (B) is the correct general category of disease but describes a single, first, active episode — before synechiae have had time to organise into the extensively lobed pattern seen here; a first acute attack more often shows circumcorneal injection and a still largely round, if sluggish, pupil.\nAcute glaucoma (C) classically presents with a fixed, mid-dilated, round (not scalloped) pupil, a hazy oedematous cornea and severe pain from a sudden pressure spike — the pupil shape in acute glaucoma is not scalloped by synechiae.\nScleritis (D) is inflammation of the sclera itself, producing a deep red or violaceous, often tender globe; it does not by itself distort the pupil margin.\nBecause the crop shows the iris/pupil only and not the full external eye, signs like ciliary injection, corneal clarity or scleral involvement that would help further stage the disease cannot be assessed from this image alone; the pupil shape is the feature the key rests on.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.151, Q2 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` (anterior uveitis sequelae: posterior synechiae, festooned pupil photographs); `ch10-uveal-tract.txt` (posterior synechiae producing irregular iris shape; cycloplegic-mydriatics to prevent/break recent synechiae).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-3', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-cornea',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the causative organism of this case?',
  options: ['Streptococcus pneumoniae','Chlamydia trachomatis','HSV','Pseudomonas aeruginosa'],
  answer: 3,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-3', imgAlt: 'Colour high-magnification anterior-segment photograph of one eye showing a hazy cornea with a whitish band inferiorly.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise severe bacterial keratitis with hypopyon and identify Pseudomonas aeruginosa as the organism most associated with rapid, aggressive corneal destruction, particularly in contact lens wearers.',
  explanation: 'The cornea in this crop is diffusely hazy with an inferior whitish-yellow band layering across the anterior chamber angle — the appearance of a hypopyon, a layer of inflammatory cells settling by gravity, seen in moderate-to-severe bacterial keratitis. The cached Cornea lecture\'s bacterial keratitis section identifies contact lens wear as the most common risk factor in the developed world and singles out Pseudomonas aeruginosa among the Gram-negative organisms as producing rapid, aggressive disease that "can cause rapid descemetocele leading to perforation within 24-48 hours" — the most severe and time-critical organism in the differential, matching the key.\nStreptococcus pneumoniae (A) is a recognised Gram-positive cause of bacterial keratitis but runs a comparatively less fulminant course than Pseudomonas and is not the organism the lecture flags for rapid 24-48 hour perforation.\nChlamydia trachomatis (B) causes follicular conjunctivitis (adult inclusion conjunctivitis, trachoma), not a hypopyon corneal ulcer — it is a conjunctival, not a corneal-suppurative, pathogen in the cached material.\nHSV (C) causes a dendritic/geographic epithelial keratitis, a branching linear lesion, not a diffusely hazy cornea with a gravitating white hypopyon band — the cached differential table separates these appearances.\nThe crop alone cannot distinguish which specific bacterium is responsible — a diffuse hazy cornea with hypopyon is compatible with several bacterial causes, and confirming Pseudomonas specifically would require a corneal scrape and culture that is not shown here; the identification rests on the classic association between this severe, rapidly destructive picture and Pseudomonas taught in the lecture, rather than on a feature unique to Pseudomonas visible in the photograph.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.151, Q3 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` (bacterial keratitis: contact lens as leading risk factor; Pseudomonas aeruginosa — rapid, aggressive, descemetocele/perforation in 24-48h; hypopyon in moderate-severe disease; bacterial vs HSV vs fungal differential).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-4', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-lid',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is The nerve supply of the affected muscle?',
  options: ['Trochlear nerve','Oculomotor nerve','Abducent nerve','Trigeminal'],
  answer: 1,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-4', imgAlt: 'Colour close-up photograph of a child\'s face showing both eyes, with one upper lid drooping compared with the other.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise unilateral ptosis and know that the levator palpebrae superioris, the muscle responsible for lid elevation, is supplied by the oculomotor nerve.',
  explanation: 'One upper lid in this photograph droops lower than its fellow, covering more of the visible eye — unilateral ptosis. The muscle that elevates the upper lid is the levator palpebrae superioris, and the cached Eyelid disease lecture\'s extraocular-muscle nerve-supply table lists this action as carried by the oculomotor (third cranial) nerve, matching the key. The same lecture\'s ptosis section illustrates oculomotor nerve palsy producing a complete unilateral ptosis, with the affected eye (once the lid is lifted) deviated "out and down" and the pupil dilated — the classic third-nerve palsy posture, consistent with a lid-drooping photograph such as this one being used to test levator innervation.\nTrochlear nerve (A) supplies only the superior oblique muscle, an extraocular muscle involved in depression and intorsion of the eye in adduction; it plays no role in lid elevation.\nAbducent nerve (C) supplies only the lateral rectus, responsible for abduction of the eye; it has no lid function.\nTrigeminal nerve (D) is predominantly sensory to the face and cornea (with a motor branch to the muscles of mastication); it does not supply the levator palpebrae superioris and is not part of the oculomotor pathway tested here.\nThe stem itself carries a mid-sentence capitalisation slip ("The nerve supply") that is transcribed as printed, being a cosmetic defect rather than a content error. Whether this particular child\'s ptosis is congenital or acquired, and whether other third-nerve signs (pupil, ocular motility) are present, cannot be determined from a face photograph alone — the question tests only the nerve supply of the muscle responsible for lid elevation, which the lecture\'s table answers directly.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.151, Q4 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` (EOM nerve-supply table); `L5) Eyelid disease` (ptosis: oculomotor paralysis — complete ptosis, eye out and down, dilated pupil on lifting the lid).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-5', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-cornea-surg',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the treatment for this condition?',
  options: ['Keratoplasty','Topical antibiotics only','Cycloplegics','Observation'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-5', imgAlt: 'Colour anterior-segment photograph of one eye showing a diffusely hazy, streaky cornea.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise diffuse, severe corneal opacification and know that keratoplasty is its treatment once the cornea is this extensively scarred.',
  explanation: 'The cornea in this photograph shows a diffuse, streaky haze spread across essentially its whole visible surface rather than a small localised scar — a severe, extensive corneal opacity of the kind that blocks vision through most of the pupillary area. The cached Cornea lecture\'s surgical section lists keratoplasty (corneal transplantation, whether penetrating or a lamellar technique such as DALK, DSAEK or DMEK) as the treatment once corneal disease has progressed to this degree of scarring or opacification, since no medical treatment can restore transparency to tissue that has already been replaced by scar. This is why the key selects keratoplasty over the non-surgical options.\nTopical antibiotics only (B) treat active bacterial infection; they cannot clear an established diffuse corneal scar, and there is no indication here of an acute infective process requiring antibiotic treatment alone.\nCycloplegics (C) relax the ciliary muscle and dilate the pupil to relieve pain and prevent posterior synechiae in anterior uveitis or acute keratitis; they have no effect on established corneal opacity and are not a treatment for a diffusely scarred cornea.\nObservation (D) would leave a vision-threatening diffuse opacity untreated indefinitely; the cached surgical indications call for active intervention once opacification is this extensive, not watchful waiting.\nThe precise underlying cause of this particular corneal haze (for example a resolved severe keratitis, a corneal dystrophy, or interstitial keratitis) cannot be determined from the photograph\'s appearance alone; the answer follows from the severity and extent of the opacity shown, which is what the lecture uses to indicate keratoplasty regardless of the exact original insult.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.152, Q5 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` (corneal surgery: keratoplasty for advanced/extensive corneal opacity).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-6', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-intro-exam',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What does this test in the photo examine?',
  options: ['Visual acuity','Retinal periphery','Corneal thickness','Intraocular pressure'],
  answer: 1,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-6', imgAlt: 'Colour clinical photograph of a standing clinician wearing a head-mounted device, leaning over a supine patient and holding a small hand-held instrument.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Identify binocular indirect ophthalmoscopy from its head-mounted-headset-plus-condensing-lens set-up and know that it examines the peripheral retina.',
  explanation: 'The photograph shows an examiner wearing a head-mounted illuminated headset and holding a small hand-held lens in front of a supine patient\'s eye — the set-up of binocular indirect ophthalmoscopy. In this technique the headset provides the examiner\'s illumination and binocular viewing system while a condensing hand lens held between examiner and patient focuses light from the fundus, giving a wide-field, stereoscopic view that reaches well out into the retinal periphery, beyond what a direct ophthalmoscope can bring into view. This is why the test examines the retinal periphery, matching key B. A small blue reference number visible in the corner of the source image is the source textbook\'s own figure-numbering box and carries no clinical meaning.\nVisual acuity (A) is tested with a letter or symbol chart at a fixed distance, not with a head-mounted headset and hand lens.\nCorneal thickness (C) is measured by pachymetry, an entirely different, non-ophthalmoscopic instrument.\nIntraocular pressure (D) is measured by a tonometer (applanation or indentation), not by a headset-and-lens ophthalmoscopy set-up.\nThe cached Ocular examination lecture covers ophthalmoscopy chiefly from the direct-ophthalmoscope perspective; the cached course textbook explicitly states that indirect ophthalmoscopy "is another instrument that is beyond the scope of this text for undergraduates," so the specific optics of binocular indirect ophthalmoscopy and its characteristic wide, peripheral field of view are drawn from general ophthalmic examination knowledge rather than the cached course material — not taken from the course material — while the identification of the equipment shown (headset plus hand lens, distinct from a direct ophthalmoscope) is a straightforward visual read of the photograph.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.152, Q6 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `ch01-examination.txt` printed p.33 (course textbook states indirect ophthalmoscopy is beyond the scope of the undergraduate text; not detailed there). General ophthalmic examination knowledge — not taken from the course material: binocular indirect ophthalmoscopy uses a headset and condensing hand lens for wide-field peripheral retinal viewing.' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-7', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-lid',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What does this Test examine?',
  options: ['Strength of the orbicularis oculi muscle','Function of the levator palpebrae superioris muscle','Corneal reflex','Extraocular muscle motility'],
  answer: 1,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-7', imgAlt: 'Two greyscale line drawings of an eye and eyelid, each showing a graduated ruler held against the upper lid margin in a different gaze position.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise the levator function (excursion) test — a ruler held against the lid between extreme downgaze and upgaze — and know it measures the function of the levator palpebrae superioris.',
  explanation: 'The two stacked drawings show the same eye and lid, each with a graduated millimetre ruler held vertically against the upper lid, with the eye directed differently between the two panels. This is the levator function (excursion) test: the examiner immobilises the brow, blocking any frontalis contribution, and measures how far the upper lid margin travels between extreme downgaze and extreme upgaze, reading the excursion in millimetres off the ruler. The cached Eyelid disease lecture teaches this exact manoeuvre by picture alone — an unheaded slide showing a before/after ruler-against-the-lid pair in downgaze and upgaze — and the surgical slides that immediately follow it use the resulting number as the threshold for choosing between levator resection (levator function 5 mm or more) and frontalis brow suspension (levator function 4 mm or less), confirming that the manoeuvre measures levator palpebrae superioris function, matching option B.\nStrength of the orbicularis oculi (A) is tested differently, by asking the patient to forcibly close the lids against the examiner\'s resistance, not by a ruler measuring lid excursion between gaze positions.\nThe corneal reflex (C) is elicited with a touch stimulus, such as a wisp of cotton, to the cornea and observing a blink, not with a ruler and gaze positions.\nExtraocular muscle motility (D) is assessed by having the patient follow a target through the cardinal positions of gaze and watching the globe move, not the lid margin, and does not use a ruler.\nBecause the illustration specifically pairs a ruler against the lid with two gaze extremes, the exact technique the lecture uses to derive the resection-versus-suspension threshold, the figure identifies the test unambiguously as a levator function assessment.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.152, Q7 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L5) Eyelid disease` (unheaded levator-function ruler slide; levator resection at 5 mm or more, frontalis brow suspension at 4 mm or less).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-8', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-glauc',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the appropriate management for this condition?',
  options: ['Topical antibiotics','Observation and follow-up','Surgical treatment','Systemic steroids'],
  answer: 2,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-8', imgAlt: 'Colour photograph of an infant\'s face, held against an adult, both eyes open, front view.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise the clinical appearance suggesting congenital (infantile) glaucoma and know that its definitive management is surgical.',
  explanation: 'The infant\'s eyes in this photograph appear prominent, with the dark central portion of each eye occupying an unusually large share of the visible palpebral fissure — an appearance consistent with buphthalmos, the enlarged globe and cornea of congenital (infantile) glaucoma, which the cached Glaucoma lecture lists among that condition\'s clinical signs, alongside a large, discoloured eye, enlarged corneal diameter, broad limbus, corneal oedema and Haab\'s striae. The same lecture states plainly that treatment of congenital glaucoma is medical only as a temporary measure until surgery, with the surgical option chosen by corneal clarity: goniotomy for a clear cornea, or trabeculotomy, trabeculectomy with antimetabolites, or synthetic valve implantation for an opaque one. Because medical therapy is never definitive, the appropriate management the key selects is surgical treatment (C).\nTopical antibiotics (A) treat infection, not raised intraocular pressure from an abnormal drainage angle; there is no infective process described or expected in congenital glaucoma.\nObservation and follow-up (B) would allow ongoing optic nerve damage from unchecked elevated intraocular pressure in a condition the lecture explicitly treats as needing surgery, not watching.\nSystemic steroids (D) have no role in lowering intraocular pressure in congenital glaucoma and are not part of its management in the cached material.\nThe photograph alone, a single front-facing image with no size reference, cannot definitively prove true corneal or globe enlargement rather than a normal infant\'s proportionally large-looking eyes; a full work-up would include corneal diameter measurement, intraocular pressure and disc assessment, none of which is available here. The diagnosis of congenital glaucoma is inferred from the option set together with the visual impression, and the surgical-treatment conclusion is directly sourced from the lecture regardless of which specific surgical technique would ultimately be chosen.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.152, Q8 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` (congenital/developmental glaucoma clinical picture — large discoloured eye, enlarged corneal diameter, broad limbus; treatment: medical temporary till surgery; goniotomy/trabeculotomy/trabeculectomy/valve).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-9', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-glauc',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What does this device examine?',
  options: ['Corneal thickness','Intraocular pressure','Visual acuity','Retinal periphery'],
  answer: 1,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-9', imgAlt: 'Colour product photograph on a plain background of a black hand-held instrument with a vertical arm, a small tip, and a graduated dial, no patient in frame.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Identify an indentation (Schiotz-type) tonometer from its footplate-and-plunger form and know it measures intraocular pressure.',
  explanation: 'The instrument photographed stands upright on a small footplate, with a slender vertical arm carrying a plunger-like tip at the top and a graduated dial with an adjustment wheel partway down the body — the classic form of a Schiotz (indentation) tonometer. The cached Clinical examination lecture describes indentation tonometry exactly this way: the plunger indents the cornea under a known weight, and the depth of indentation, read off the calibrated scale, is converted to an intraocular pressure reading, alongside the alternative method of applanation tonometry (for example, Goldmann). Because indenting the cornea under a known weight is what this device does, it examines intraocular pressure, matching key B.\nCorneal thickness (A) is measured by pachymetry, a different instrument (an ultrasonic probe or optical device) that does not have a footplate-and-plunger form and does not carry a Schiotz-style dial.\nVisual acuity (C) is assessed with a chart, not a hand-held mechanical instrument placed on the eye.\nRetinal periphery (D) is examined with an ophthalmoscope, direct or indirect, not an indentation device resting on the cornea.\nThe instrument\'s own printed lettering is too small to read at this resolution, so the specific model cannot be confirmed from the crop, but the overall shape — footplate, central plunger, calibrated scale and adjustment knob — is distinctive enough among ophthalmic instruments to identify it as an indentation tonometer without needing the label; that identification, and the fact that it measures intraocular pressure, both come directly from the cached examination lecture\'s description of Schiotz tonometry.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.153, Q9 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` (indentation tonometry, Schiotz: indentation of the cornea by a known weight; applanation vs indentation methods).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-10', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-cornea-surg',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the treatment in an advanced case of this condition?',
  options: ['Topical antibiotics','keratectomy','Keratoplasty','Spectacles'],
  answer: 2,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-10', imgAlt: 'Colour photograph cropped to the upper face, both eyes, brows and bridge of the nose, front view.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise bilateral corneal clouding and know that keratoplasty is the treatment once corneal opacity is advanced.',
  explanation: 'Both corneas in this crop show a visible bluish-grey clouding over their central/visible area rather than the normal dark pupil showing through clearly — a bilateral corneal opacity. When corneal clouding is mild, spectacles, topical treatment, or a lesser surgical procedure directed at the corneal surface may suffice; but once the opacity is advanced and involves enough of the visual axis to compromise vision, only replacing the diseased cornea restores clarity, which is what keratoplasty (corneal transplantation) does. The cached Cornea lecture lists keratoplasty among the corneal-surgery answers to disease that has reached this stage, distinct from the non-surgical or lesser-surgical options offered for earlier disease, which is why option C is keyed for the "advanced case" specifically.\nTopical antibiotics (A) treat active infection; a chronic bilateral opacity like this is not, by itself, evidence of an active infective process, and antibiotics cannot clear an established corneal scar or opacity.\nKeratectomy (B), surgical removal of superficial corneal tissue, addresses more superficial pathology, for example a superficial scar or an anterior corneal dystrophy; it is not adequate once opacity is advanced and involves deeper corneal layers, which is the distinction the stem\'s word "advanced" is testing.\nSpectacles (D) can correct a refractive error but cannot restore transparency to a cornea that is opacified; light still cannot pass clearly through a clouded cornea no matter how it is refracted.\nThe specific underlying diagnosis producing the bilateral haze, for example a corneal dystrophy or the sequela of earlier bilateral keratitis, is not identifiable from the photograph\'s appearance alone; what determines the answer is the severity and advanced stage of the opacity, which the option set is built to test rather than the exact original cause. Option B is printed lower-case in the source and is transcribed as printed.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.153, Q10 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` (corneal surgery section; keratoplasty for advanced/severe corneal opacity, as distinct from lesser surgical or non-surgical options).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-11', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-conj',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the treatment for this ocular condition?',
  options: ['A single dose of azithromycin','Erythromycin 500 mg twice daily for 7 days','doxycycline 500 mg twice daily for 10 days','Topical 1% tetracycline ointment is highly effective here'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-11', imgAlt: 'Colour high-magnification photograph of one eye with the upper eyelid everted over a retractor, the tarsal conjunctival surface in view.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise follicular conjunctivitis of the everted tarsal conjunctiva as adult chlamydial (inclusion) conjunctivitis and identify a single dose of azithromycin as its treatment of choice.',
  explanation: 'The everted upper-lid tarsal conjunctiva shown is diffusely red and studded with numerous discrete, pale, raised follicles across most of its surface — a cobblestoned, granular texture rather than a smooth pink surface. The cached Conjunctiva lecture and book chapter both illustrate adult chlamydial (inclusion) conjunctivitis with exactly this picture: an everted-lid photograph of discrete follicles on a red tarsus, caused by Chlamydia trachomatis serovars D to K, with mucopurulent discharge and tender preauricular nodes as accompanying signs. The book\'s treatment section for this condition lists systemic antibiotics, azithromycin, doxycycline or erythromycin, and, per the trachoma page\'s dosing detail, states that "a single dose of azithromycin (20 mg/kg up to 1 g) is the treatment of choice," matching option A exactly, including the phrase "single dose".\nErythromycin 500 mg twice daily for 7 days (B) is close to but not the book\'s regimen: the cached material gives erythromycin 500 mg twice daily for 14 days, not 7 — the duration printed in this distractor is wrong for the taught regimen, and in any case a multi-dose course is a less convenient alternative to the single-dose azithromycin regimen specifically named "treatment of choice".\nDoxycycline 500 mg twice daily for 10 days (C) prints the wrong dose: the cached regimen is doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 10 days, not 500 mg — 500 mg twice daily is a markedly supratherapeutic dose for doxycycline and is not the course-taught figure, beyond doxycycline again being a multi-day alternative rather than the single-dose treatment of choice.\nTopical 1% tetracycline ointment "is highly effective here" (D) overstates what the source says: the book states topical tetracycline is less effective than oral treatment for this condition, so calling it highly effective directly contradicts the cached material, quite apart from oral therapy being preferred overall.\nThe staging\'s own printed doses (500 mg, 7 days, 10 days, 1%) are transcribed as printed even where they diverge from the cached regimen — the divergence is the distractor design, not a transcription fault, and is flagged here because it involves doses and durations, per this bank\'s rule that numeric discrepancies are always noted.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.153, Q11 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L6) Conjunctiva` (chlamydial conjunctivitis / trachoma slide, everted-lid follicles); `ch06-conjunctiva.txt` printed p.92-93 (adult chlamydial conjunctivitis treatment: azithromycin/doxycycline/erythromycin named; trachoma dosing — azithromycin single dose 20 mg/kg up to 1 g, erythromycin 500 mg twice daily for 14 days, doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 10 days, topical 1% tetracycline less effective than oral).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-12', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-cornea',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the most likely cause of this corneal condition?',
  options: ['Bacterial infection','Fungal infection','Herpes simplex virus','Allergic reaction'],
  answer: 2,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-12', imgAlt: 'Colour slit-lamp anterior-segment photograph of one eye at high magnification, cornea, iris and pupil in view.', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise a branching, dendritic corneal lesion and identify herpes simplex virus as the classic cause.',
  explanation: 'The lower-central cornea in this crop shows a faint but distinctly branching, tree-like (dendritic) linear pattern with a slightly pink tint against the clearer surrounding cornea. The cached Cornea lecture describes this exact appearance as the hallmark of herpes simplex epithelial keratitis, a classic dendritic ulcer with terminal buds, staining with fluorescein in the ulcer bed and Rose Bengal at the virus-laden margins, and lists HSV as the most common infectious cause of corneal blindness in developed countries. This branching shape is specific enough on its own to identify the cause as herpes simplex virus, matching key C.\nBacterial infection (A) typically produces a more solid, greyish stromal infiltrate with an epithelial defect and circumcorneal injection, often with hypopyon in moderate-to-severe cases, a diffuse or focal infiltrate rather than a fine branching linear pattern; the lecture\'s differential table places the dendritic/geographic ulcer specifically under herpes simplex, contrasted against bacterial keratitis.\nFungal infection (B) classically produces feathery, satellite lesions with a dry, rough surface and, often, hypopyon, again a different, more irregular and feathery morphology than a clean branching dendrite, and typically follows a slower onset with a history of plant-matter trauma, neither of which is shown here.\nAllergic reaction (D) does not produce a corneal dendrite or any corneal ulcer at all; allergic eye disease is a conjunctival process (papillae, itching, mucoid discharge) and does not explain a branching corneal lesion.\nTerminal bulbs at the ends of the branching lines, the feature that would make the dendrite diagnosis fully certain on fluorescein staining, cannot be confirmed at this photograph\'s resolution, and the extent of the lesion beyond the visible frame is not knowable from the crop; but the branching shape itself, as taught in the cached lecture, is distinctive enough to select herpes simplex virus over the other three listed causes.\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.153, Q12 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` (HSV epithelial/dendritic keratitis — classic dendritic ulcer with terminal buds; bacterial vs HSV vs fungal differential table).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-13', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-lac',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the main complaint of this patient?',
  options: ['Photophobia', 'Epiphora', 'Blurred vision', 'Eye redness'],
  answer: 1,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-13', imgAlt: "Colour external photograph of an older adult's eyes, cropped to both eyes, brows, nose bridge and upper cheeks, front view, lids open", imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Epiphora is tear overflow from either hypersecretion or defective lacrimal drainage; matted, discoloured material pooling at the medial canthus in an older patient is the kind of sign that accompanies it.',
  explanation: "**Epiphora is the overflow of tears at the lid margin, and it has two mechanisms.** The lacrimal deck splits it cleanly: (1) hypersecretion, secondary to anterior-segment disease such as dry eye (\"paradoxical watering\") or surface inflammation, and (2) defective drainage — from punctal malposition such as ectropion, obstruction anywhere along the drainage pathway, or lacrimal-pump failure from lower-lid laxity or orbicularis weakness. Either route ends the same way: tears that should drain down the nasolacrimal duct sit on the lid instead.\n\n**What the photo shows, and how far it goes.** At the inner corner of one eye there is a patch of yellow-green discoloration, with the adjacent lower lid margin looking slightly more irritated than the fellow side. That is consistent with matted tears or mucoid material collecting at the punctal/canthal area — a plausible accompaniment of epiphora in an older patient (lid malposition and drainage obstruction both become commoner with age). What the still photograph cannot show is the more direct sign of epiphora — visible wetness or an overflow trail on the cheek — so the image supports the keyed complaint without demonstrating it outright. Nothing in the crop points more strongly at one of the other three options.\n\n**Why the distractors do not fit better.** *Photophobia* is light-averse squinting or reflex lid closure — the eyes here are open and the gaze is unremarkable, with nothing suggesting glare avoidance. *Blurred vision* would need a media opacity — corneal haze, cataract, or a retinal problem — and the visible cornea and pupil are not described as abnormal. *Eye redness* is present only as mild irritation confined to one lid margin, not the diffuse conjunctival injection that would make redness itself the chief complaint; the canthal discoloration is the more specific finding, and it points at the lacrimal system rather than at surface inflammation.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.154, Q13 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L9) lacrimal system.` slides 9-10 (epiphora definition and its two mechanisms, hypersecretion vs. defective drainage).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-14', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-cornea',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'Regarding the picture shown, which of the following is true?',
  options: ['There is a clear zone between the arcus and the limbus', 'The opacity involves the entire cornea including the center', 'It is a sign of acute corneal infection', 'It indicates elevated intraocular pressure'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-14', imgAlt: 'Colour anterior-segment photograph of one eye at high magnification, lids apart, lashes above and below, cornea, iris and pupil in view', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Arcus senilis is a peripheral corneal lipid arc, physiological in the elderly, separated from the true limbus by a thin clear band, and it spares the visual axis.',
  explanation: "**This is the one question in the section where the options describe the picture rather than name a diagnosis, and the photo answers both halves at once.** A grey-white ring of opacity sits around the peripheral cornea, concentric with the limbus — but between that ring and the limbus itself there is a visible band of clearer cornea, a gap rather than the ring sitting flush against the sclera. That gap is option A. At the same time, the central cornea over the pupil stays clear, with the pupil plainly visible through it — which directly contradicts option B (\"the opacity involves the entire cornea including the center\"). The photo is doing double duty: it confirms A and it rules out B in the same frame.\n\n**What this finding is.** `L15,16) Cornea` lists it among the age-related corneal degenerations: \"Arcus senilis (lipid arc at limbus — physiological in elderly, check lipids if <45 yrs).\" It is a peripheral lipid deposit, essentially a corneal analogue of arcus that accompanies ageing (and, below 45, a reason to check a lipid profile). ⚠️ **The specific detail the options hinge on — that a thin clear band of cornea (the interval of Vogt) separates the lipid arc from the true limbus** — is not spelled out in the cached slide, which gives the entity and its clinical significance but not this anatomical detail; that clear zone is standard descriptive teaching for arcus senilis and is marked here as **not taken from the course material**.\n\n**Why C and D fail.** *Acute corneal infection* would need conjunctival injection, epithelial defect, or an infiltrate — none of which the photograph shows; a peripheral, symmetric, chronic-looking ring is the opposite of an acute picture. *Elevated intraocular pressure* is not read off a corneal ring at all — raised IOP shows itself in the disc, the angle, or acute corneal oedema (a diffusely hazy, not ringed, cornea), none of which this crop displays.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.154, Q14 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` corneal degenerations list (arcus senilis, physiological in the elderly). The clear-zone-of-Vogt detail is not taken from the course material.' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-15', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-lid',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the appropriate treatment for this case?',
  options: ['Topical antibiotic drops', 'Incision and curettage', 'Oral corticosteroids', 'Cryotherapy'],
  answer: 1,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-15', imgAlt: 'Colour external photograph of one eye and its brow at high magnification, front view, lids apart', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Incision and curettage through the conjunctival (tarsal) side is the standard surgical treatment of a chalazion once it has not resolved with conservative measures.',
  explanation: "**Chalazion is a sterile, chronic granulomatous inflammation of a meibomian gland**, presenting as a gradually enlarging, painless, firm nodule within the tarsal plate. `ch05-eyelids.txt` p.73 gives the treatment ladder directly: warm compresses with massage for early lesions, then surgical treatment — intralesional steroid injection, or **incision and curettage through the conjunctival side** — with a recurrent chalazion, especially in an older patient, biopsied to exclude meibomian gland carcinoma. Incision and curettage is the option printed here, and it is the standard operative answer for a chalazion that has not settled conservatively.\n\n**⚠️ WHERE THE CROP AND THE KEY PART WAYS.** At the resolution available in this crop, no discrete raised nodule or lid-margin swelling is clearly identifiable — the one conspicuous feature is a mottled, stippled iris surface, which is not a lesion incision and curettage would ever treat. This is noted rather than resolved: either the actual chalazion sits outside this tight crop's framing (higher on the lid or brow, cropped away), or the crop simply does not resolve a lesion that is genuinely there. The printed key is shipped as printed regardless — a defective or inconclusive crop is not grounds to move an answer — and the reasoning above rests on what the stem and treatment options are built around (a chalazion) rather than on a lesion visibly confirmed in this particular image.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong for a chalazion.** *Topical antibiotic drops* treat infective, not granulomatous, disease — a chalazion is sterile inflammation, not an infection, so antibiotics do not address the underlying blocked gland. *Oral corticosteroids* are not part of the standard chalazion ladder at all; a localised lesion like this is managed locally (intralesional steroid, not systemic) or surgically, and systemic steroids carry side effects out of proportion to a single lid nodule. *Cryotherapy* is not a listed treatment for chalazion in the cached material — it belongs to other lid and ocular-surface conditions, not to draining a blocked meibomian gland.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.154, Q15 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `ch05-eyelids.txt` p.73 (chalazion definition and treatment ladder: compresses, intralesional steroid, incision and curettage through the conjunctival side, biopsy of a recurrent case).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-16', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-conj',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the most common symptom here?',
  options: ['Redness', 'Discharge', 'Itching', 'Pain'],
  answer: 2,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-16', imgAlt: 'Colour external photograph of one eye with the lid everted, high magnification, the conjunctival surface occupying the upper part of the frame and the lower cornea and pupil visible below', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'A dense field of large, flat-topped, cobblestone papillae on the everted tarsal conjunctiva is the giant papillary appearance of vernal keratoconjunctivitis, in which itching is the dominant symptom.',
  explanation: "**The everted conjunctiva here is covered edge to edge with large, flat-topped, closely packed polygonal elevations — a cobblestone mosaic of giant papillae**, not the smaller, scattered follicles of an infective picture. `L6) Conjunctiva.` slide 38 shows exactly this appearance under the heading Vernal KeratoConjunctivitis: \"the tarsal conjunctiva is covered by LARGE, FLAT-TOPPED, POLYGONAL GIANT PAPILLAE packed edge to edge in a cobblestone mosaic.\" `ch06-conjunctiva.txt` gives the symptom list for this allergic group directly: intense itching, painless tearing, diffuse redness, mucoid/ropy discharge, papillae, lid oedema and chemosis — with itching named first and, for vernal keratoconjunctivitis specifically, described as \"Severe itching\" ahead of photophobia and the ropy discharge. Itching is the symptom classically taught as dominant in this group, matching the printed key.\n\n**Why the other three, though all real findings in this disease, are not the \"most common\" symptom.** *Redness* is listed as diffuse but is not the symptom the teaching foregrounds — the same source calls the itching \"intense\"/\"severe\" while redness sits further down the list. *Discharge* is present as a mucoid, ropy strand, but it is a sign patients report as secondary to the itching-driven eye rubbing, not the presenting complaint itself. *Pain* is not a feature of allergic conjunctivitis at all in the cached material — the condition is uncomfortable and itchy, not painful, and marked pain in a red eye instead points toward a different differential (keratitis, uveitis, acute glaucoma) that this picture does not otherwise support.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.154, Q16 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L6) Conjunctiva.` slide 38 (giant papillae, Vernal KeratoConjunctivitis); `ch06-conjunctiva.txt` p.87-88 (allergic conjunctivitis symptom list; VKC \"Severe itching\").' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-17', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-trauma',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'Identify the ocular finding shown in the photo:',
  options: ['Vossius ring', 'Kayser-Fleischer ring', 'Fleischer ring', 'Hudson-Stahli line'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-17', imgAlt: 'Very high-magnification anterior-segment photograph, dark field, with a bright arc-shaped reflection visible against it', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'A Vossius ring is a partial, irregular, brownish ring of iris pigment stamped onto the anterior lens capsule by the pupil margin after blunt ocular trauma.',
  explanation: "**A thin, irregular, rust-orange/brown arc — incomplete rather than a full circle — is the shape a Vossius ring classically takes.** `L25) Trauma I` lists it directly under blunt trauma (slide 30/31, \"Vossious ring\" — the deck's own spelling variant of Vossius — with a photograph of a \"pigment ring on the anterior lens capsule\"), and `L18) Cataract II`'s traumatic-cataract table tabulates \"Vossius ring (blunt)\" as the sign of a blunt-trauma cataract, alongside a rosette-shaped cataract from the same mechanism. The pigment comes from the posterior iris epithelium: a blunt blow drives the iris back against the lens, and the pupillary margin stamps its pigment onto the anterior capsule, leaving a broken, roughly pupil-sized ring rather than a smooth, complete circle — which is exactly the irregular arc shape in this crop, favouring the printed key.\n\n**Why the other three rings/lines do not fit.** *Kayser-Fleischer ring* is a copper deposit in Descemet's membrane at the corneal limbus (Wilson's disease) — a corneal, not a lenticular, sign, and classically a more complete circumferential ring rather than a broken arc. *Fleischer ring* is an iron deposit at the base of the cone in keratoconus, again corneal and typically a more regular ring around the cone base. *Hudson-Stahli line* is a horizontal iron line across the lower-to-mid cornea in normal ageing, a straight line rather than an arc, and unrelated to trauma. All three sit on the cornea; the finding this question describes belongs on the anterior lens capsule instead.\n\n**Uncertain, noted rather than resolved.** The crop is tightly framed and does not include the pupil margin or the limbus, so the anatomical plane of the arc (lens capsule versus cornea) cannot be independently confirmed from the image alone; the identification rests on the arc's colour and broken shape, which match a Vossius ring and do not match any of the three corneal alternatives.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.155, Q17 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L25) Trauma I` slide 30/31 (Vossius ring, blunt trauma); `L18) Cataract II` traumatic-cataract table (Vossius ring listed as the sign of blunt-trauma cataract).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-18', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-neuro-optic',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What type of optic atrophy is associated here?',
  options: ['Primary optic atrophy', 'Secondary optic atrophy', 'Consecutive optic atrophy', 'Papilledema-induced optic atrophy'],
  answer: 2,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-18', imgAlt: 'Colour dilated fundus photograph, round field filling the frame, optic disc left of centre', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Consecutive optic atrophy shows a waxy pale disc with markedly attenuated vessels against a diffusely mottled retina — atrophy consecutive to a primary retinal disease process such as retinitis pigmentosa.',
  explanation: "**The disc here is pale with blurred margins, set against a retina that shows diffuse, fine, grainy pigmentary mottling rather than a smooth reddish-orange background — and that pairing is what separates consecutive atrophy from the other three types.** `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 14 lays out the four-way classification by photograph alone: primary atrophy is chalky white with sharp margins and a normal retina; secondary is dirty grey-white with blurred margins and a mottled peripapillary halo (the residue of preceding swelling); glaucomatous shows pallor confined to a deep cup; and **consecutive** shows a waxy yellow disc with markedly attenuated, thread-like vessels against a pale, washed-out retina — the retinal disease that caused the atrophy visible in the background. Slide 16 extends the same pattern with peripheral bone-spicule pigment clumps, naming it \"the CONSECUTIVE pattern... shown with its cause visible.\" The diffuse pigmentary mottling in this crop is that retinal-disease signature, and it is what points to option C.\n\n**Why the other three do not fit as well.** *Primary optic atrophy* needs a chalky-white disc with sharp margins over an otherwise normal retina — the mottled background here rules that out. *Secondary optic atrophy* is the pattern that follows disc swelling itself (papillitis or papilledema), with a grey-white disc and peripapillary residue, not the diffuse pigmentary retinal disease seen here. *Papilledema-induced optic atrophy* is a chronic end-stage of active disc swelling, and there is no elevated, oedematous disc in this photograph — only a flat, pale one.\n\n**A caveat honestly carried forward.** The grainy, stippled quality of the retinal background could reflect genuine pigmentary retinal disease or, in part, photographic grain at this resolution — the two cannot be fully separated from the crop alone, though the overall pattern still reads most consistently with the consecutive type.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.155, Q18 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 14 (four-type optic-atrophy classification by photograph) and slide 16 (consecutive pattern with peripheral pigment clumps).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-19', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-neuro-optic',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'Identify the condition shown in the photo:',
  options: ['Primary optic atrophy', 'Secondary optic atrophy', 'Consecutive optic atrophy', 'Papilledema-induced optic atrophy'],
  answer: 3,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-19', imgAlt: 'Two colour fundus photographs printed side by side, disc-centred views of both eyes', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Chronic papilledema, left untreated, progresses from active bilateral disc swelling to bilateral optic atrophy; the photographs here catch the swollen, hyperaemic, blurred-margin stage that precedes it.',
  explanation: "**Both frames show a raised, whitish-yellow, heaped disc with lost margins — bilateral, and matching one eye against the other rather than one photograph against a caption.** `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 6, captioned \"Papilledema\", shows exactly this pairing: two fundus photographs of one patient's two eyes, used specifically to make the point that papilledema is bilateral (an orange fundus, hyperaemic heaped disc, lost margins, engorged tortuous vessels, flame haemorrhages at the disc margin in the fuller original, radiating exudate). The crop here is a smaller, less florid version of the same picture — elevated whitish-yellow discs, blurred margins, radiating folds, bilateral and symmetric — which is the signature of disc oedema from raised intracranial pressure, not of any of the atrophy patterns in options A–C.\n\n**Why the key is D and not the others.** *Primary optic atrophy* (option A) needs a flat, chalky-white disc with sharp margins — nothing elevated or swollen. *Secondary optic atrophy* (option B) and *consecutive optic atrophy* (option C), as this same deck's slide 14 draws them, are both late, pale, atrophic end-stages — dirty grey-white with a peripapillary halo for secondary, waxy yellow with attenuated vessels over a diseased retina for consecutive — and neither shows the disc still actively elevated and hyperaemic, which is what both photographs here show.\n\n**An honest overlap worth naming.** This course's own slide 14 classifies the chronic, pale atrophy that follows resolved papilledema or papillitis AS \"secondary optic atrophy\" — so strictly, \"papilledema-induced optic atrophy\" (option D) and \"secondary optic atrophy\" (option B) describe the same downstream endpoint in that scheme, and a stricter question would have made D the correct label for what B is really naming. **The printed key is shipped as D regardless** — a defective or overlapping option label is noted, never disputed. What tips the answer toward D rather than B in practice is that this photograph shows *active* papilledema, not the atrophic sequela either label technically means; the question is best read as asking the student to name the disease process on view (papilledema) rather than pick between two atrophy sub-types neither photograph actually shows.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.155, Q19 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 6 (bilateral papilledema, paired fundus photographs) and slide 14 (atrophy classification, for contrast against B and C).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-20', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-conj',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the treatment of choice for this case to prevent recurrence?',
  options: ['Cryotherapy', 'Excision with bare autograft', 'Topical steroids', 'Simple excision'],
  answer: 1,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-20', imgAlt: 'Colour external photograph of one eye at high magnification, lids apart, cornea and adjacent bulbar conjunctiva in view', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'A pterygium excised with the bare-sclera technique alone recurs in roughly a third of cases; adding a conjunctival autograft at the harvested bed lowers that recurrence to about 5%, which is what "to prevent recurrence" is asking for.',
  explanation: "**A pinkish, fleshy, triangular growth with visible fine vessels crosses from the bulbar conjunctiva toward the cornea, apex pointed corneal-ward** — the wing shape (Greek *pterygos*) is the finding the whole question is built on, and `ch06-conjunctiva.txt` p.95 opens with the same description: \"fibrovascular, triangular, wing-like encroachment of epithelial tissue onto the cornea\", adding that it can reduce vision by inducing astigmatism or by covering the pupillary axis.\n\n**Why B is the key.** The same page states the recurrence numbers directly: simple excision with bare sclera leaves the bed exposed and recurs in about **one-third** of cases; adding a **conjunctival autograft** at that bed (harvested from the superior bulbar conjunctiva, per the page's figure) drops recurrence to about **5%**. Since the stem specifically asks for the option that prevents recurrence, and not simply treatment, the graft is what the question is testing. (The option's own wording, \"excision with bare autograft\", compresses \"bare sclera\" and \"conjunctival autograft\" into one phrase — the two are opposite technique names in the source and the option is reproduced exactly as printed; it is read here as intending excision-plus-autograft, the technique the page credits with the lower recurrence.)\n\n**Why the distractors are wrong.** *Cryotherapy* (A) is not the management this page lists for pterygium at all — cryotherapy has a role elsewhere in ophthalmology (e.g. retinal breaks, some tumours) but is not part of the pterygium ladder given here; **not taken from the course material** beyond that negative. *Topical steroids* (C) have a role for irritative symptoms or post-operative inflammation, which the page allows (\"irritative symptoms may be treated with lubricating drops\"), but they do not excise the lesion and do nothing to prevent surgical recurrence. *Simple excision* (D) is the bare-sclera technique itself — effective at removing the lesion, but it is exactly the technique the page credits with the higher, one-third recurrence rate, the opposite of what the stem asks for.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.155, Q20 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `ch06-conjunctiva.txt` p.95 (pterygium definition, bare-sclera vs. conjunctival-autograft recurrence rates, mitomycin-C alternative).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-21', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-cornea',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'What is the efferent nerve of this reflex?',
  options: ['Oculomotor nerve', 'Trigeminal nerve', 'Facial nerve', 'Optic nerve'],
  answer: 2,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-21', imgAlt: "Colour external clinical photograph of a woman's head and shoulders, facing the camera, a gloved hand holding a folded tissue near the outer part of one eye", imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'The corneal (blink) reflex has the trigeminal nerve as its sensory/afferent limb and the facial nerve as its motor/efferent limb, closing the lids via orbicularis oculi.',
  explanation: "**A gloved hand brings a folded tissue up to the eye — the technique for eliciting the corneal reflex, touching the cornea to provoke a bilateral blink.** That reflex arc has two separate cranial nerves on its two limbs: sensation from the cornea travels in the ophthalmic division of the **trigeminal nerve (CN V1)**, the afferent side, while the blink itself is produced by orbicularis oculi, supplied by the **facial nerve (CN VII)**, the efferent side. The stem asks specifically for the efferent nerve, which is the facial nerve — matching the printed key.\n\n**Why the distractors are wrong.** *Oculomotor nerve* (A) supplies the levator palpebrae superioris (lid elevation) and the extraocular muscles that move the globe, not the reflex blink closure being tested here. *Trigeminal nerve* (B) is the correct nerve for this reflex, but for its **afferent**, not efferent, limb — the question specifically asks for the efferent side, so B names the right reflex and the wrong limb. *Optic nerve* (D) carries vision, not corneal touch sensation or eyelid motor output, and plays no role in this particular reflex arc at all.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.156, Q21 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Precedent: `opqb-t8-329` (efferent limb of corneal sensitivity testing, facial nerve).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-22', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-neuro-motil',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'Which done first in this patient?',
  options: ['Blood sugar level', 'Lipid profile', 'CT', 'Visual Field assessment'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-22', imgAlt: 'Two colour external photographs stacked one above the other, each cropped to both eyes and the surrounding face, front view', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'An acquired sixth (abducent) nerve palsy in an adult over 55 is most often microvascular — from diabetes or hypertension — and its work-up starts with the simple, fast systemic tests (blood sugar) before imaging is pursued.',
  explanation: "**Both photographs show one eye deviated outward relative to its fellow across what reads as an attempted gaze series** — the pattern of a motility deficit rather than a static structural finding, and the two-photo layout (matching the deck's habit of pairing gaze positions to demonstrate a limitation) points toward an ocular motor palsy being tested here. Esotropia in primary position with limited abduction of the affected eye and horizontal diplopia that worsens on looking toward the paretic side are the textbook triad of a **sixth (abducent) nerve palsy** — `ch14-neuro-ophthalmology.txt` p.184 sets it out exactly this way, adding the compensatory head turn toward the side of the lesion.\n\n**Why blood sugar comes first.** The same page's aetiology list splits by age: compressive lesions such as an intracranial tumour account for about 20% of cases and are relatively more common under 45; **microvascular infarction of the nerve, as in diabetes and hypertension, is the most common cause above 55**, and it typically resolves spontaneously within 3–4 months without any intervention beyond managing the underlying disease. The page's own investigation list opens with exactly this logic — \"systemic assessment: blood sugar level, glycosylated haemoglobin, full lipid profile, blood pressure measurement\" — before it turns to imaging. A quick fingerstick or fasting glucose is fast, cheap, and directly tests the statistically likeliest cause first, which is what \"done first\" is asking for; the stem does not give an age, but the systemic-first-then-image order the page teaches is a general rule for isolated motor nerve palsies presenting this way, not one tied to a specific age cutoff shown in the photo.\n\n**Why the distractors are wrong, or wrong to do first.** *Lipid profile* (B) is a systemic risk-factor test the same page lists alongside blood sugar, but it is not the fastest or first-line screen for an acute palsy — blood sugar/glucose is checked before a fasting lipid panel in ordinary practice. *CT* (C) is the page's recommended investigation \"especially in children and young adults,\" where compressive causes are relatively more likely; in an older patient with an isolated palsy and no other red flags, imaging is reserved for cases that fail the expected spontaneous recovery, or for younger patients, rather than being the first step. *Visual field assessment* (D) tests a different system altogether — it evaluates the optic nerve/visual pathway, not ocular motility, and has no bearing on diagnosing or working up a sixth nerve palsy.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.156, Q22 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `ch14-neuro-ophthalmology.txt` p.184 (sixth nerve palsy: clinical triad, aetiology by age, investigation order).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-23', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-acute',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'A patient present with sudden painless loss of vision. Fundus picture shows this finding below. The treatment includes:',
  options: ['Anterior chamber (AC) paracentesis', 'PRP', 'C)Anti-VGEF', 'Topical steroids'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-23', imgAlt: 'Colour fundus photograph of one eye, round field, optic disc to the right of the frame with the macular area left of centre', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Central retinal artery occlusion presents with sudden painless loss of vision, retinal whitening and a cherry-red spot at the macula; anterior chamber paracentesis is an emergency measure aimed at acutely lowering intraocular pressure to try to dislodge the occluding embolus and restore flow.',
  explanation: "**A pale, washed-out retinal background with a small, distinctly reddish spot standing out at the centre of the macula is the classic cherry-red-spot appearance**, and `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` lists exactly that combination as the sign set of central retinal artery occlusion: VA below 6/60, a marked afferent pupillary defect, diffuse retinal whitening, a cherry-red spot at the macula, and arteriolar/venular narrowing with segmentation of the blood column (\"cattle-trucking\"). Paired with a stem describing sudden, painless vision loss, this is CRAO, matching the printed key of anterior chamber paracentesis.\n\n**A caveat carried forward honestly.** The retinal vessels visible in this particular crop read as roughly normal calibre rather than the visibly attenuated, thread-like arterioles a textbook CRAO photograph usually shows; that single feature is inconclusive at this resolution and could reflect an early or partial occlusion, a cropping limit, or simply photographic quality — it does not outweigh the cherry-red-spot/pale-retina pattern that is otherwise a strong match for the stem and the key, so the printed key is shipped as given.\n\n**Why option A is correct, and why this is a gap-fill.** `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` presents a CRAO case in full but leaves its \"Management\" slide entirely blank — verified across the whole deck, with the specific absence noted in the deck's own appendix: no ocular massage, no anterior chamber paracentesis, no IOP-lowering drops, no rebreathing, no time window is printed anywhere in the course material. **This treatment is answered from general medical knowledge and tagged accordingly — not taken from the course material**: anterior chamber paracentesis acutely lowers intraocular pressure, which can shift or dislodge an embolus lodged in the central retinal artery and is a recognised (though time-limited and often unsuccessful) emergency measure for CRAO, alongside ocular massage and IOP-lowering drops, consistent with the same gap-fill already used for the CRAO precedent `opqb-t13-524`.\n\n**Why the distractors are wrong.** *PRP* (panretinal photocoagulation, B) treats retinal neovascularisation from chronic ischaemia (e.g. proliferative diabetic retinopathy or ischaemic CRVO), not an acute arterial occlusion. *Anti-VEGF* (C, printed \"C)Anti-VGEF\" as in the source) suppresses vascular endothelial growth factor-driven neovascularisation or macular oedema — again a chronic-ischaemia or exudative-disease treatment, not an emergency measure for restoring flow in an acute occlusion. *Topical steroids* (D) treat ocular inflammation and have no role in an arterial occlusion, which is a perfusion emergency rather than an inflammatory process.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.156, Q23 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` (CRAO sign list, cherry-red spot); `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` (CRAO case presented, Management slide blank — gap noted in the deck appendix). AC paracentesis is not taken from the course material. Precedent: `opqb-t13-524` (CRAO treatment options, same gap-fill).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-24', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-ret',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'A Fundus picture shows this pic. What is the most likely complaint?',
  options: ['Flashes and floaters', 'Gradual painless vision loss', 'Eye pain and redness', 'Double vision'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-24', imgAlt: 'Colour fundus photograph of one eye, round field, optic disc right of centre, dimmer and lower-contrast than other fundus photographs in this section', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment presents with sudden floaters, flashes of light, and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral field — a symptom triad distinct from the gradual, painless course of the tractional and exudative types.',
  explanation: "**The frame reads noticeably duller and hazier than the other fundus photographs in this section, with a pale-yellow disc and radiating vessels** — a quality consistent with an elevated or detached segment of retina scattering light differently from flat, attached retina, though no discrete fold, demarcation line or tear is resolvable in this crop, so the read leans on the image's overall dulled quality rather than a sharp structural sign. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 11 gives the symptom table this question is built on: **rhegmatogenous retinal detachment (RRD)** presents with floaters, flashes of light, and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision, and its onset is \"often sudden and rapid\" — matching option A and the printed key. Slide 12's fundus figure pairs the same symptom set with a pale, elevated quadrant against otherwise orange attached retina, which is the general appearance this photograph's duller region is consistent with.\n\n**Why the distractors describe the other patterns instead.** *Gradual painless vision loss* (B) is the symptom set the same table gives to **tractional retinal detachment**, which presents with gradual loss and distorted or wavy vision as scar tissue slowly pulls the retina — a different mechanism (diabetic fibrovascular traction) and a different photographic appearance (white fibrotic bands, dot-blot haemorrhages, neovascularisation) from what a rhegmatogenous detachment shows. *Eye pain and redness* (C) is not a symptom the slide assigns to any of the three detachment types — retinal detachment is characteristically painless regardless of type, since the retina itself has no pain fibres; pain and redness point toward an anterior-segment or inflammatory process rather than a posterior-segment detachment. *Double vision* (D) is a symptom of ocular misalignment or motility disturbance, not of a retinal detachment, and is not part of the deck's detachment symptom table at all.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.156, Q24 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 11 (RD symptom table by type) and slide 12 (fundus appearance of the three types).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-25', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-lid',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'A patient present with this eye Picture. What clinical feature helps in diagnosis?',
  options: ['Glued eyes upon waking', 'Sudden flashes of light', 'Painful eye movements', 'Night blindness'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-25', imgAlt: 'Colour external photograph of one eye at high magnification, front view, lids apart', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise meibomian gland dysfunction/blepharitis as the cause of a lid margin that glues shut overnight.',
  explanation: '**The waxy yellow-white deposits capping the lid margin in the photograph are meibomian gland plugs, and a lid margin choked with inspissated secretion is exactly what leaves the lids glued together on waking.**\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 26 (Posterior Blepharitis / Meibomian Gland Dysfunction) photographs this precise appearance — pale yellow-white plugs capping the meibomian orifices along the posterior lid margin, with granular material coating the adjacent lashes. Meibomian gland dysfunction thickens and disrupts the lipid layer the glands normally secrete onto the tear film; overnight, with the lids closed and no blink to disperse it, that abnormal secretion dries and mats the lid margins and lashes together — the patient wakes with lids that must be prised or bathed open, the "glued eyes" of option A. *The precise phrase "glued eyes upon waking" as a named clinical feature is not printed verbatim in the cached deck* — the underlying mechanism is, and the phrasing itself is standard bedside teaching, tagged **not taken from the course material**.\n\n**Why the other three do not fit a lid-margin photograph:**\n- *Sudden flashes of light (option B)* is a photopsia — a vitreoretinal traction symptom, the retina firing because something is pulling on it (posterior vitreous detachment, a retinal tear). It has nothing to do with the anterior lid margin the photograph shows.\n- *Painful eye movements (option C)* points to the orbit or the optic nerve sheath — classically optic neuritis, where movement stretches an inflamed nerve. The photograph shows the lid margin, not the globe\'s motility or the optic disc.\n- *Night blindness (option D)* is a retinal/photoreceptor symptom — rod dysfunction, as in retinitis pigmentosa or vitamin A deficiency. It is not read off an external lid photograph at all.\n\nNone of the distractors is a lid-margin sign; only crusting/gluing belongs to the picture in front of the examiner.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.157, Q25 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L5) Eyelid disease` sl.26 (posterior blepharitis / meibomian gland dysfunction — pale yellow-white plugs capping the meibomian orifices along the posterior lid margin). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT, not repaired: stem prints "A patient present with" for "presents", and capitalises "Picture" mid-sentence. As printed.' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-26', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-orbit-mass',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'A patient presents withCT scan shows the following. What finding is expected on examination?',
  options: ['Binocular diplopia', 'Hypoesthesia of forehead', 'Orbital cellulitis', 'Painless central vision loss'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-26', imgAlt: 'Greyscale coronal CT of the head showing both orbits and the paranasal sinuses', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Link a coronal orbital-floor CT with the diplopia an orbital blow-out fracture produces on examination.',
  explanation: '**A coronal CT through the orbits is the classic view for an orbital floor ("blow-out") fracture — and diplopia, not the other three, is what that fracture produces on examination.**\n\n`ch03-orbit.txt` p.58–59 puts the mechanism and the finding together: a blunt object larger than the orbital aperture (fist, ball, an airbag or steering-wheel impact) drives the globe backward and ruptures the thin floor of the orbit into the maxillary sinus below — the asymmetric sinus change coronal CT is used to show. The same page states plainly that coronal CT is important to diagnose floor fractures and tissue (inferior oblique muscle) entrapment, and that diplopia follows from two mechanisms: haemorrhage/oedema restricting globe movement (which resolves as the swelling settles), and mechanical entrapment of the inferior oblique or inferior rectus within the fracture line, which produces diplopia in both upgaze and downgaze and does not resolve without surgery.\n\n**Why the other three do not fit:**\n- *Hypoesthesia of forehead (option B)* is the sensory territory of the supraorbital nerve (frontal branch of V1) — a roof, not a floor, injury. A floor fracture instead numbs the infraorbital nerve\'s territory (cheek, upper lip, upper teeth), not the forehead, so option B names the wrong nerve for this fracture.\n- *Orbital cellulitis (option C)* is an infective, not a traumatic, diagnosis — it needs fever, lid erythema and proptosis with pain on movement, none of which follows mechanically from a fracture on its own.\n- *Painless central vision loss (option D)* is a posterior-segment/optic-nerve symptom; a floor fracture\'s damage is confined to the orbital walls and the extraocular muscles it entraps, not to central retinal or optic-nerve function.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.157, Q26 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `ch03-orbit.txt` p.58–59 (orbital floor blow-out fracture mechanism, coronal CT diagnosis, entrapment-driven diplopia); `L25) Trauma I` (orbital floor fracture coverage). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT, not repaired, verified at 3x magnification: "withCT" is printed as one word with no space and the sentence is malformed — "A patient presents withCT scan shows the following." As printed; the missing words are not supplied.' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-27', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-acute',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'A patient present with this fundus Which investigation is most appropriate?',
  options: ['Carotid Doppler', 'B Scan', 'Optical coherence tomography (OCT)', 'MRI of the orbit'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-27', imgAlt: 'Colour fundus photograph of one eye', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Pair a cherry-red-spot CRAO fundus with carotid Doppler as the standard embolic work-up.',
  explanation: '**A pale, ischaemic fundus with a comparatively reddish macular spot is the cherry-red-spot appearance of a retinal arterial occlusion, and once that diagnosis is on the table, carotid Doppler is the standard next step to look for the embolic source.**\n\n`L31,32) Acute visual Loss` builds Case 1 around this exact picture — sudden painless monocular vision loss, a relative afferent pupillary defect, and a white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot, diagnosed as central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO). CRAO in an older patient is most often embolic, and the carotid artery is the commonest source of that embolus — hence carotid Doppler ultrasound, looking for a stenotic or ulcerated plaque that could be showering emboli into the ophthalmic circulation. *This step of the CRAO work-up is not itself printed in the cached deck, which stops at the diagnosis slide and leaves the management slides blank* — the reasoning above is standard teaching for CRAO, tagged **not taken from the course material**.\n\n**Why the other three do not fit:**\n- *B Scan (option B)* is for when the fundus cannot be visualised directly — a hazy media (dense cataract, vitreous haemorrhage). Here the retina is clearly photographed, so there is nothing for a B scan to add.\n- *Optical coherence tomography (option C)* images retinal/macular structure layer by layer — useful for macular disease, not for finding the embolic source of an arterial occlusion that has already declared itself on the fundus photograph.\n- *MRI of the orbit (option D)* looks at orbital soft tissue and the optic nerve sheath, not at the carotid circulation feeding the retinal artery; it is the wrong study for an embolic work-up.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.157, Q27 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` Case 1 (CRAO — white opacified retina with cherry-red macular spot; management/work-up not printed in the deck, filled from standard teaching). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT, not repaired: no sentence break between the two clauses — the stem runs "with this fundus Which investigation", and prints "present" for "presents". As printed.' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-28', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-glauc',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'Treatment of this condition includes all of the following EXCEPT:',
  options: ['Pilocarpine', 'Panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)', 'Anti-VEGF injections', 'Control of underlying Cause'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-28', imgAlt: 'Colour anterior-segment photograph of one eye at high magnification', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Identify pilocarpine as inappropriate in neovascular glaucoma, unlike PRP, anti-VEGF, and treating the underlying ischaemia.',
  explanation: '**Treatment of neovascular glaucoma is built around treating the retinal ischaemia driving new-vessel growth — PRP, anti-VEGF and correcting the underlying cause are all appropriate; pilocarpine is the one that does not belong, which is what the EXCEPT is fishing for.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` places neovascular glaucoma among the secondary angle-closure mechanisms: a neovascular membrane grows across the trabecular meshwork and contracts, pulling the peripheral iris over the drainage angle and closing it. Panretinal photocoagulation (option B) and anti-VEGF injection (option C) both target the retinal ischaemia that stimulates that neovascular membrane in the first place — ablating the ischaemic retina, or blocking the VEGF signal it releases, so the vessels regress and the angle stops being pulled shut. "Control of underlying cause" (option D) is the umbrella instruction covering both, plus treating whatever ischaemic disease (commonly proliferative diabetic retinopathy or a retinal vein occlusion) triggered the process. *Why pilocarpine specifically is unhelpful is not spelt out in the cached deck* — standard teaching, tagged **not taken from the course material**, is that miotics like pilocarpine increase pupillary block and ciliary congestion and can worsen inflammation and angle closure in a neovascular eye, and they do nothing for the ischaemic drive behind the new vessels, which is why they are avoided rather than prescribed.\n\n**A note on the image:** the anterior-segment photograph does not resolve fine new vessels on the iris surface at this magnification (rubeosis iridis, the specific sign that would confirm the diagnosis outright) — the answer here rests on the option pattern matching standard neovascular glaucoma teaching, not on a visually confirmed finding of iris neovascularisation.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.157, Q28 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` (neovascular glaucoma as secondary angle closure; PRP/anti-VEGF target the ischaemic drive). Pilocarpine\'s specific unsuitability is standard teaching, not printed in the deck — tagged in the explanation. ⚠️ SOURCE CASING, not repaired: option D prints "Control of underlying Cause" with a mid-phrase capital C. As printed.' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-29', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-cat-surg',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'These glasses are used for correction of:',
  options: ['Aphakia', 'Astigmatism', 'Amblyopia', 'Myopia'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-29', imgAlt: 'Colour external photograph of an infant wearing spectacles, head and shoulders, front view', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise thick convex aphakic spectacles and the surgical history they imply.',
  explanation: '**Thick, strongly convex spectacle lenses of the kind magnifying this infant\'s eyes are the classic correction for aphakia — an eye with no crystalline lens at all, almost always here from paediatric cataract surgery without a primary intraocular lens.**\n\n`L18) Cataract II` tables aphakia directly: after lens removal the eye is left roughly +10 to +12 D hypermetropic, corrected with aphakic spectacles, contact lenses, or a secondary IOL, and specifically notes that in paediatric aphakia a contact lens is preferred under about two years of age with a secondary IOL implanted once ocular growth has stabilised. A lens strong enough to correct +10–12 D is thick and highly convex, which is exactly why, viewed from outside, it magnifies the appearance of the wearer\'s eyes the way this photograph shows — a cosmetic, external effect distinct from the lecture\'s separate note that, for the wearer looking through such a lens, the image itself carries a ring scotoma from peripheral prismatic distortion and significant magnification of the visual image, which is why unilateral aphakia is poorly tolerated in spectacles and a contact lens or secondary IOL is preferred once only one eye is affected.\n\n**Why the other three do not fit:** astigmatism (option B) is corrected with a cylindrical, not a uniformly thick spherical, lens and produces no such magnified appearance; amblyopia (option C) is a cortical deficit treated by patching or penalisation, not by any specific spectacle design; myopia (option D) is corrected with concave (minus) lenses, which minify rather than magnify the appearance of the eyes behind them — the opposite of what the photograph shows.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.158, Q29 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L18) Cataract II` (aphakia table — +10 to +12 D hypermetropia after lens removal, aphakic spectacles vs contact lens vs secondary IOL, paediatric aphakia management, ring scotoma and image magnification for the wearer).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-30', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-glauc',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'This visual field defect is characteristic of which disease?',
  options: ['Glaucoma', 'Papilledema', 'Optic neuritis', 'Retinitis pigmentosa'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-30', imgAlt: 'Black-and-white visual field chart, a polar grid with one shaded defect region plotted on it', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Match an arcuate/Bjerrum visual field defect to glaucomatous nerve fibre bundle loss.',
  explanation: '**A curved, arc-shaped shaded field defect sweeping around fixation is the arcuate (Bjerrum) scotoma, and it is the visual field signature the module attaches specifically to glaucoma.**\n\n`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lists the open-angle glaucoma field defects in an explicit order: paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum\'s area, arcuate scotomata (superior, inferior), a nasal step, a ring scotoma (a double arcuate scotoma), progressing to constriction of the field and eventually a temporal island of vision in end-stage disease. These defects trace the arcuate course of the retinal nerve-fibre bundles converging on the optic disc, so a nerve-fibre bundle lost to glaucomatous cupping produces this hooked, disc-related shape rather than a defect centred anywhere else.\n\n**Why the other three do not fit:**\n- *Papilledema (option B)* classically produces an enlarged blind spot, from swelling of the disc itself pressing outward on the surrounding retina — a defect centred on the blind spot, not arcing around fixation.\n- *Optic neuritis (option C)* typically produces a central or caecocentral scotoma, because the inflammation strikes the papillomacular bundle carrying central vision, the opposite pattern from an arcuate defect that spares fixation until very late disease.\n- *Retinitis pigmentosa (option D)* produces a ring scotoma in the mid-periphery that progresses to peripheral constriction, from the outside in as rod photoreceptors die first — a pattern that starts and stays peripheral, rather than tracing a nerve-fibre bundle in toward the disc.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.158, Q30 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` (glaucomatous field defect sequence — paracentral/Bjerrum, arcuate, nasal step, ring scotoma, end-stage constriction).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-31', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-orbit',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'This device is used to measure:',
  options: ['Proptosis', 'Intraocular pressure', 'Corneal curvature', 'Pupil size'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-31', imgAlt: 'Colour photograph of a clinical examination in progress: an instrument held horizontally across a patient\'s face at the level of the outer orbital rims', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Identify an exophthalmometer by its rim-to-rim positioning and know it measures proptosis.',
  explanation: '**A horizontal bar instrument with a graduated scale held across the face at the level of the lateral orbital rims is an exophthalmometer, and what it measures is proptosis — how far forward the globe sits relative to the orbital rim.**\n\n`L10) orbit.txt` slide 8 names the instrument directly — the Hertel exophthalmometer, used with visualisation from above — and gives the numbers that make the reading useful: a value greater than 20 mm indicates proptosis, and a difference of 2–3 mm or more between the two eyes is suspicious regardless of the absolute reading, because it flags asymmetry even when both eyes fall inside a normal range individually.\n\n**Why the other three do not fit:** intraocular pressure (option B) is read off a tonometer applied to the cornea itself (applanation or indentation), not a bar held across the orbital rims; corneal curvature (option C) is measured with a keratometer, which projects a target onto the cornea and reads its reflection — an entirely different optical principle from a mechanical rim-to-rim bar; pupil size (option D) is read directly with a ruler or pupil gauge against the eye itself, not with an instrument spanning both orbital rims. None of those three instruments takes its reading from the lateral orbital margins the way the pictured device does.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.158, Q31 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L10) orbit.txt` sl.8 (Hertel exophthalmometer — measurement of proptosis, >20 mm abnormal, 2–3 mm interocular asymmetry suspicious).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-32', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-pupil',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'Spot diagnosis:',
  options: ['Rt Optic Neuritis', 'Lt optic Neuritis', 'Ocuulmotor Palsy'],
  answer: 0,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-32', imgAlt: 'Colour schematic drawing of four eyes in a 2x2 grid, with a small torch drawn beaming at one eye of each row', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Read a swinging-flashlight illustration as a relative afferent pupillary defect and attribute it to optic neuritis rather than to a third nerve palsy.',
  explanation: '**The drawing is a swinging-flashlight test. In the upper row the torch is on one eye and *both* pupils are small; in the lower row the torch has swung to the fellow eye and *both* pupils are large. A pupil pair that dilates when the light arrives on one particular eye is a relative afferent pupillary defect — the Marcus Gunn pupil — and among the three options only optic neuritis produces one.**\n\n`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 6 shows this exact sequence as four stacked photographs of one patient captioned "Marcus Gunn Pupil": baseline both pupils small and equal; torch on one eye and both dilate; torch on the fellow eye and both constrict; torch back again and both dilate. The deck reads it out for you — the eye whose illumination *dilates* both pupils is the defective one, because its damaged optic nerve carries less light signal to the midbrain than the fellow nerve does, so swinging the torch onto it is effectively a reduction in stimulus and both pupils relax. `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 34 lays out the same test as a two-column table — under "Normal" the pupil *constricts*, under "relative afferent pupillary defect" the pupil *dilates* — and slide 32 lists the swinging-flashlight test among the steps of a routine pupil examination. `L21) Optic Nerve` then attaches the sign to the nerve and to the disease. Slide 3, "Manifestations of Optic Nerve Dysfunction", prints five items, and RAPD is one of only two carrying a red star — the deck\'s way of flagging the signs that *localise* a lesion to the optic nerve, as against the reduced acuity, colour vision and contrast that merely accompany it. Slide 8\'s handwritten papilloedema-versus-papillitis table then grades it: the RAPD row holds three ticks in the papillitis column and the word "No" in the papilloedema column. *(Papillitis is optic neuritis at the disc; the deck uses the two names in the two places — "Acute optic neuritis" on slide 3, "Papillitis" on slides 7 and 8 — and never states the equivalence, so that link is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n*The drawing carries no R/L marker, no arrow, no face and no orientation cue of any kind, so which of the two drawn eyes is the patient\'s right cannot be read off the picture — that depends on whether the artist drew the patient facing you (mirrored) or from the patient\'s own side. The printed key says the right eye, and the key is recorded as printed. The teaching point the question is testing — this pattern means a relative afferent pupillary defect, therefore optic neuritis — does not depend on that convention, and options A and B differ only in it.*\n\n**Why C does not fit:** a third nerve palsy damages the *efferent* limb, and `L30) Squint II` slides 11–13 give its three classic components together — ptosis, an eye deviated "down and out", and a dilated pupil on that side. That pupil is fixed and dilated whichever eye the torch is on; it does not swing. The drawing shows neither ptosis nor any deviation of the drawn eyes, and its pupils change together in both rows rather than one staying large — the opposite of what an oculomotor palsy looks like.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.158, Q32 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. THREE options only (A–C) as printed. Source defects kept verbatim: "Ocuulmotor" (doubled u) in option C, and the inconsistent casing of "Rt Optic Neuritis" / "Lt optic Neuritis". Supporting: `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` sl.6 (Marcus Gunn pupil — the four-frame swinging-flashlight series); `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` sl.32, sl.34 (swinging-flashlight test; RAPD dilates where normal constricts); `L21) Optic Nerve` sl.3 (RAPD starred among the manifestations of optic nerve dysfunction), sl.8 (papilloedema-vs-papillitis table — RAPD "No" vs three ticks); `L30) Squint II` sl.11-13 (ptosis + down-and-out + dilated pupil in third nerve palsy).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-33', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-trauma',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'Which of the following is most likely associated with this type of lesion?',
  options: ['Blunt trauma by airbag', 'A broken glass', 'Chemical burn', 'Both A & B'],
  answer: 3,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-33', imgAlt: 'Colour external/anterior-segment photograph of one eye at high magnification, under strong specular reflection', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Recognise a full-thickness open-globe wound with uveal prolapse as following from either blunt or sharp trauma.',
  explanation: '**A curved dark structure crossing the lower field, with a rounded, smooth, blue-grey mass bulging out beside it, reads as tissue prolapsing through a break in the globe wall — and that pattern of injury follows from blunt rupture and from sharp laceration alike, which is why "Both A & B" is the answer rather than either mechanism alone.**\n\n`L25) Trauma I` draws exactly this distinction: rupture globe is caused by a blunt object (the wall gives way from a sudden rise in intraocular pressure, tearing outward at its weakest points — often at the limbus or under the rectus insertions), while laceration, partial or full thickness, is caused by a sharp object. An airbag deploying into the face is a classic blunt mechanism capable of producing a rupture; a shard of broken glass from a windscreen or a struck window is a classic sharp mechanism capable of producing a laceration — and once either wound is full-thickness, uveal (iris/ciliary body) tissue can prolapse through it in the same way, blunt and penetrating rupture looking alike once that has happened. *At this magnification the exact identity of the dark linear structure (wound margin vs lid margin) and of the blue-grey mass (prolapsed iris vs other tissue) cannot be pinned down with full confidence* — the photograph supports "globe rupture with tissue prolapse" as the injury category, compatible with either mechanism, without specifying which one actually caused this particular wound. The deck also lists the signs that should raise suspicion of an open globe before this stage is reached — a peaked pupil, a shallow or deep anterior chamber, bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage, and a positive Seidel test for leaking aqueous — and warns that a suspected open globe should be shielded, never patched, with pressure and further examination withheld until the ophthalmologist takes over.\n\n**Why C alone does not fit:** a chemical burn (option C) injures the ocular surface — cornea and conjunctiva — by direct toxic/alkali or acid contact, not by a full-thickness mechanical wound; it does not produce a prolapsing mass of the kind this photograph shows, which is why it is excluded even though the question allows a combined answer for the other two.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.158, Q33 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L25) Trauma I` (globe rupture from blunt trauma vs laceration from a sharp object; open-globe warning signs; shield-not-patch management).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-34', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-trauma',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'A 25-year-old male presents after a workplace accident with sudden onset of pain and decreased vision. A CT orbit is shown below. Most likely diagnosis:',
  options: ['Retrobulbar Hge', 'Rt orbital abscess', 'Intraorbital foreign body', 'Rt orbital hematoma'],
  answer: 2,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-34', imgAlt: 'Greyscale axial CT of the head at the level of the orbits, bone window, both globes in view', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Choose the diagnosis a CT is ordered to make after a workplace penetrating injury, and know why CT is the imaging of choice for a suspected foreign body.',
  explanation: '**A workplace accident with sudden pain and lost vision, worked up with a CT of the orbit, is the standard presentation of a penetrating foreign body — and CT is precisely the study ordered when one is suspected.**\n\n`L25) Trauma I` slide 52 is the relevant slide: if a foreign body is suspected, investigation may include plain skull films, ultrasound, and a CT scan, which the deck says is done to determine the location and presence of a metallic foreign body and to show the shape and integrity of the traumatised globe — with an explicit warning in capitals not to perform MRI where a metallic foreign body is suspected, because a ferromagnetic fragment can be moved by the magnet. Slides 53–54 carry the figure that makes it visual: "Metallic IOFB ??? → CT ✓ · MRI ✗", printed beside axial CT images. Slide 56 lists mechanism of injury as the first item of the focused trauma history, and a workplace accident in a young man is the classic high-velocity setting (hammering, grinding, drilling) in which a fragment is driven into the eye or orbit.\n\n*Read at full magnification the scan is a bone-window axial cut through both orbits, with the nasal septum, ethmoid air cells and orbital walls printing bright; a discrete hyperdense fragment separate from normal bone could not be resolved at the printed resolution, so this entry rests the answer on the clinical stem and on the investigation the deck ties to it rather than on a fleck identified in the figure.* That does not change the key, which is recorded as printed.\n\n**Why the other three do not fit:** an orbital abscess (option B) is an infective complication — `L10) orbit.txt` slides 28–29 put it in the setting of bacterial orbital cellulitis, typically spreading from ethmoid sinusitis, investigated with white cell count, blood cultures and high-resolution CT of orbit, sinuses and brain, and treated with admission and intravenous antibiotics; it does not appear *suddenly* on the day of an injury, and the stem gives no fever, no lid erythema and no sinus disease. Options A and D are, on any ordinary reading, the same entity written twice — a retrobulbar haemorrhage *is* an orbital haematoma — and two options that cannot be told apart from each other cannot be the intended single key; a bleed behind the globe also presents with tense proptosis and a rising intraocular pressure rather than as an imaging finding a CT is ordered to hunt for. *Retrobulbar haemorrhage / orbital compartment syndrome is not taught as a named entity in this course — `L25) Trauma I` does not name it and `L10) orbit.txt` mentions "retrobulbar hge" only in passing as a cause of proptosis — so the reasoning that separates A and D from each other is **not taken from the course material**.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.159, Q34 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Option abbreviations kept as printed ("Retrobulbar Hge", "Rt orbital abscess", "Rt orbital hematoma"). Supporting: `L25) Trauma I` sl.52 (suspected foreign body — x-ray, CT, ultrasound; CT locates a metallic FB and shows globe integrity; MRI contraindicated), sl.53-54 ("Metallic IOFB ??? → CT ✓ · MRI ✗"), sl.56 (mechanism of injury heads the trauma history); `L10) orbit.txt` sl.28-29 (orbital cellulitis with subperiosteal abscess from ethmoid sinusitis; investigations and intravenous treatment), sl.6 (retrobulbar haemorrhage listed only as a cause of proptosis).' },

{ id: 'opqb-t26-35', module: 'ophtho', chapter: 'op-insid',
  bank: 'gradegain',
  stem: 'The most useful investigation to detect the cause of vision loss in this patient is:',
  options: ['US', 'OCT', 'Filed Examination', 'ESR'],
  answer: 1,
  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-35', imgAlt: 'Colour fundus photograph of one eye', imgEssential: true,
  objective: 'Select OCT as the investigation that structurally characterises macular/RPE pathology on fundus photography.',
  explanation: '**A mottled, streaky mix of pale and reddish-brown retinal areas radiating out from the disc is the kind of retinal pigment epithelium disturbance that optical coherence tomography exists to characterise layer by layer, which is why OCT is the most useful investigation here rather than the other three.**\n\n`L35,36) Chronic visual loss` names age-related macular degeneration as a leading cause of irreversible central visual loss in older patients, and separates its two forms by exactly this kind of RPE change: dry disease (the great majority of cases) as slow progressive atrophy of the RPE and photoreceptors with drusen, and wet disease (a minority) as RPE detachment and choroidal neovascularisation. Both produce visible RPE-level mottling on fundus photography of the kind in this image, and OCT is the tool that turns that photographic appearance into the cross-sectional detail — fluid, RPE elevation, or a neovascular membrane — that actually distinguishes dry from wet disease and therefore drives management (supplements/monitoring for dry disease against anti-VEGF injection for wet, per the same lecture). *The photograph\'s exact underlying diagnosis is not confidently readable at this resolution* — the streaky, geographic pattern of the paler areas would also be consistent with a condition such as angioid streaks, which the cached deck does not teach; only the general category — a structural retinal/RPE abnormality that OCT is built to characterise — is supported by the image, tagged **not taken from the course material** where it goes beyond that general category.\n\n**Why the other three do not fit:** ultrasound (option A) is reserved for when the fundus cannot be seen directly, which is not the case here — the retina is clearly photographed; visual field testing ("Filed Examination", option C, as misprinted in the bank) measures function, not the retinal structure the stem is asking to characterise; ESR (option D) is a systemic inflammatory marker, relevant to an arteritic cause of vision loss, not to imaging a structurally abnormal macula.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  source: 'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.159, Q35 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 6 (Photos)"), key p.159. Supporting: `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` (age-related macular degeneration — dry vs wet, RPE/drusen changes, OCT-guided management); `op-insid.draft.js` (op-insid confirmed as the only ophtho deck teaching AMD). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT, not repaired, verified at 3x magnification: option C prints "Filed Examination" for "Field Examination". As printed.' }
